course: biology
lesson: 330
title: Unusual pets: stick insects, giant snails, ant farms and hermit crabs
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Not every pet has fur. Stick insects, giant snails, ants and hermit crabs live in tanks and jars, and each one is a small window into how animals survive.
explain: A stick insect sways like a twig in the wind and drops to the floor stiff as a stick when touched. A giant snail rasps through cucumber with a tongue of thousands of tiny teeth. Ants carry their dead to a far corner to keep germs away.
explain: Picture a beach where hermit crabs line up by size beside one big empty shell. The biggest crab moves in, the next takes its old shell, and so on down the queue, like families moving into bigger houses one after another.
explain: These pets come with one rule: never let them go outside. Released pets can die or become pests, and many pests around the world began as someone's unwanted pet.
fact: Stick insects rock like twigs in the wind so hunters ignore them, they eat leaves such as bramble or privet, and many pet stick insects lay eggs that hatch without a father.
fact: A stick insect has six legs, like every insect, and can drop a leg if grabbed; a young one can grow it back, often smaller, when it next sheds its skin, but grown-ups no longer shed.
fact: A stick insect that falls and lies stiff is playing dead so a hunter loses interest, and some stick insects are among the longest insects in the world, over half a metre with legs out.
fact: Giant African land snails can grow to about 20 centimetres; they are banned in some countries because escaped snails eat crops and spread fast, and hands are washed after holding them.
fact: Snails have a ribbon-shaped tongue covered in thousands of tiny teeth that wear down and are replaced by new rows, and some snails have over 10,000 teeth.
fact: An ant colony needs a queen to lay eggs or it slowly dies out, and ants from shops without a queen last only a few months.
fact: Dead ants give off a smell that tells others to carry them out, which keeps germs away from the nest; scientists dabbed that smell on living ants and their sisters carried them out too.
fact: Hermit crabs have soft back ends and borrow empty snail shells, so as they grow a tank needs spare shells, and painted shells sold in shops can harm crabs.
fact: Wild hermit crabs sometimes queue by size and swap shells one after another, each moving into the shell left by the next biggest; scientists call this a vacancy chain.
fact: A hermit crab has ten legs, but the back ones are small and hidden inside the shell to hold on, so only six are easy to see.
fact: Hermit crabs need damp air and company because they live in groups, and with good care some are reported to live over 20 years.
fact: Pets let go can die or become pests that harm wild animals, and rescue centres and pet shops can take unwanted pets back.
exercise: Why does a stick insect sway on its branch? | It rocks like a twig in the wind so hunters ignore it | It is dancing slowly from side to side to attract a mate | It is too weak to stand still | It is trying to shake off leaves
exercise: What do pet stick insects eat? | Leaves such as bramble or privet | Meat scraps from the kitchen, like ham and chicken | Small flies they catch | Seeds and nuts
exercise: How many legs does a stick insect have? | Six, like every insect | Eight, like a garden spider | Four, like a cat or dog | Hundreds, like a millipede
exercise: When can a young stick insect grow back a lost leg? | When it next sheds its skin | The very same day it lost it | Only when it lays its first eggs | Never, a lost leg is gone for ever
exercise: Why does a stick insect fall and lie stiff when touched? | It is playing dead so a hunter loses interest | It has fainted from fright and will never get up again | It is going to sleep for winter | It is trying to look for food on the floor
exercise: How long can a giant African land snail grow? | About 20 centimetres | About 2 millimetres, like a garden snail's baby | About 2 metres, like a door | About 1 metre, like a dog
exercise: Why are giant African land snails banned in some countries? | Escaped snails eat crops and spread fast | They are too slow to cross roads and cause traffic jams | They bite people's toes | They make the rivers salty
exercise: What makes the rasping sound when a snail eats cucumber? | A ribbon tongue covered in thousands of tiny teeth | Two big front teeth like a rabbit's, gnawing at the skin | Its shell scraping on the plate | Its feet stamping on the food
exercise: How many teeth can some snails have? | Over 10,000 | Exactly 2 teeth | About 32, like us | None at all, ever
exercise: Why is the queen the most important ant in an ant farm? | Without a queen to lay eggs, the colony slowly dies out | She is the biggest, so she guards the door and fights off every enemy | She is the only ant that can dig | She tells the others jokes
exercise: Why do ants carry their dead to one corner? | To keep germs away from the nest | To bury them as food for the queen | To make a tall pile to climb up on | Because dead ants are too heavy to move
exercise: How do ants know an ant is dead? | Dead ants give off a special smell | They see that it stops moving | The queen tells them | They hear its heart stop
exercise: Why does a hermit crab live in an empty snail shell? | Its back end is soft and needs protecting | It ate the snail that lived there and kept its home | Its own shell fell off | It likes the colours of the shell
exercise: What must a hermit crab tank always have? | Spare empty shells of bigger sizes | Painted shells from the shop | Only one shell for all the crabs | No shells, so the crabs grow faster
exercise: What is a vacancy chain in hermit crabs? | Crabs queue by size and each moves into the shell left by the next biggest | Crabs leave the beach in a long line at night, each holding the next one's shell | Crabs hold claws in a long line to walk | Crabs fight until only one is left
exercise: How many legs does a hermit crab have? | Ten, though only six are easy to see | Six, like an insect | Eight, like a spider | Four, like a dog
exercise: What should you do with a pet you can no longer keep? | Take it to a rescue centre or pet shop that takes pets back | Put it out in the garden and let it fend for itself in the hedges | Let it go in the park | Leave it at the bus stop
test: Count them: 5 stick insects sit on a twig. How many legs in all? | 30 | 40 | 20 | 25
test: Count them: 3 hermit crabs in a tank. How many legs really, and how many are easy to see? | 30 legs, with 18 easy to see | 18 legs, with 18 easy to see | 30 legs, with 30 easy to see | 24 legs, with 18 easy to see
test: In A Bug's Life the ants walk on two legs and use two arms like people. Count them: how many legs does each real ant have, and how many would 4 real ants have? | 6 each, so 24 | 4 each, so 16 in all | 8 each, so 32 in all | Only 2 each, so 8
test: In SpongeBob, Mr. Krabs walks on two thin legs. How many legs does a real crab of his kind have, counting the claws? | 10 | 12 | 6 | 8
test: «me mate's gonna let his snails go in the park, they'll be happier» What is the best answer? | Released pets can die or become pests; take them to a rescue or pet shop | Fine, pet snails always do much better out in the wild than in any tank | Great, snails love parks | Only if he lets them go at night
test: «got ants from the shop, no queen, they'll be fine» What will happen? | Without a queen laying eggs, the colony will die out in a few months | They will choose a new queen from a worker in a day | They will live for ever without one | The biggest ant will lay all the eggs
test: Scientists dab the «dead ant» smell on a living ant. What happens? | Its sisters carry it out to the dead pile even though it is alive | Nothing, because ants judge death by looking, not by smell | The ant dies at once | The queen eats it
test: In a hermit crab queue by size, what would happen if the biggest crab did not move into the new shell? | Nobody else could move up, and the whole chain would stop | The smallest crab would take the big shell | All the crabs would get new shells anyway | The shell would grow to fit the next crab
test: «grown-up stick insect lost a leg, it'll grow back next time it sheds» What is wrong? | Grown-up stick insects no longer shed, so the leg will not grow back | Nothing is wrong, every stick insect regrows any lost leg each week of its life | Stick insects never lose legs | Legs grow back only on the head
test: «shop's got pretty painted shells for my crab, buying 'em» What is the best advice? | Painted shells can harm crabs; offer plain empty shells | Painted shells keep crabs healthy | Crabs only move into painted shells | The paint makes crabs grow faster
test: Why would a snail with only a few big teeth have trouble? | Its teeth wear down, so thousands of small ones replaced in rows work better | A few big teeth would let it chew through metal and stone, which would hurt it | Big teeth would make it too fast | Snails do not need to eat at all
test: A stick insect is half a metre long with its legs out. Why might a bird still miss it? | It looks and sways like a twig, so the bird ignores it | Birds cannot see anything long and thin, only round things | It hides under water | It sings to scare birds
exam: Identify the reason for which a stick insect falls and remains motionless when disturbed. | Feigning death causes a predator to lose interest | The insect has suffered a permanent injury | The insect is commencing hibernation | The insect is seeking food upon the ground
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the removal of dead members from an ant colony. | Dead ants emit a chemical signal that prompts others to remove them, thereby protecting the nest from disease | The dead are removed solely because they are visibly motionless, ants recognising death by sight alone | The dead are removed solely to provide food for the queen | Ants make no provision for their dead
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vacancy chain observed among hermit crabs? | Crabs assemble by size and each occupies the shell vacated by the next larger crab | Crabs engage in combat until a single individual remains | Crabs construct new shells from sand | Crabs exchange shells at random without regard to size
exam: Ascertain the appropriate course of action in respect of a pet which can no longer be kept. | It is to be surrendered to a rescue centre or pet shop which accepts such animals | It is to be returned to the wild in the region from which its species originally came | It is to be released into a public park | It is to be abandoned at a public place
